  The way to solve the problem in this case is to 
``````
Count the number of sequences length 10 where residual length of sequences all ones is not greater than two.
i.e., count the number of sequences length 10 where the number of 0-1-0 subsequence is not greater than two.


`````
Answer: You can easily realize that if at least 3 consecutive houses don't receive newspaper and when you got to the 3rd house, the sequence up to that house must "tighten" as much as possible, i.e., 010, thus breaking the three consecutive rules.
Therefore you can break into two cases:
Case 1: when the sequence starts with "0", you only need to consider the number of ways add four additional houses to the sequence 0.
Case 2: when the sequence starts with "01", you only need to consider the number of ways add three additional houses to the sequence 01.

```
Code:
```cpp

#include <bits/stdc++.h>

using namespace std;

const int N = 10 + 5;

int main() {
    vector<vector<int>> dp(N, vector<int>(3, 0));

    dp[0][0] = 1;
    dp[0][1] = 1;
    dp[0][2] = 1;

    for (int i = 1; i < N; i++) {
        for (int j = 0; j <= 2; j++) {
            dp[i][0] += dp[i - 1][j];
            dp[i][j] += dp[i - 1][(j + 1) % 3];
        }
    }

    cout << dp[N - 1][0] + dp[N - 1][1] + dp[N - 1][2] << endl;

    return 0;
}

The output result is:

Case 1: the number of ways add 4 additional houses to the sequence "0"
Case 2: the number of ways add 3 additional houses to the sequence "01"
